WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:08.497
Art + Design = BFFs, Best Friends Forever,

2
00:00:08.497 --> 00:00:16.147
or Queering the Pedagogical Framework for

3
00:00:16.147 --> 00:00:24.170
Interdisciplinarity
between Art and Design.

4
00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:26.450
Art (Rachel Merrill): So my name is Art.

5
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.240
Design (Cyndi Wiley):
And my name is Design.

6
00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.530
And together we’re BFFs.

7
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.250
We’re here to dispel the myth that
art and design can’t be close friends,

8
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:58.040
that they should remain separate
as two different ways of thinking.

9
00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:03.860
You can comment on this discussion
by using the hashtag #downwithsilos.

10
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.800
How are BFFs relevant to
teaching art and design?

11
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.760
A colloquial term in the United States for
women who are very close friends is BFF.

12
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.310
BFF is an acronym meaning best friends
forever and is characterized typically by

13
00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:22.920
teenage girls and young women in junior
and senior high school relationships.

14
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.033
As the term BFF has been
appropriated into popular culture and

15
00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:31.611
is now used by varying ages and
genders, we are further appropriating

16
00:01:31.611 --> 00:01:35.260
the term to queer conventional
art and design pedagogy.

17
00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:39.203
This pedagogical framework
provides an innovative melding of

18
00:01:39.203 --> 00:01:42.396
traditional disciplines,
encouraging experimentation by

19
00:01:42.396 --> 00:01:45.821
students from constant and
consistent exposure to differing

20
00:01:45.821 --> 00:01:49.420
viewpoints and critical lenses
within the classroom dichotomy.

21
00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.131
The collaborative approach provides
interdisciplinarity between

22
00:01:53.131 --> 00:01:56.960
art and design that segues
contemporary art and design practices.

23
00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:02.626
The pedagogical framework provides
divergent ideological basis for design

24
00:02:02.626 --> 00:02:07.407
and art faculty to work together
throughout a student’s full course of

25
00:02:07.407 --> 00:02:12.189
undergraduate study by team teaching,
sharing technological resources,

26
00:02:12.189 --> 00:02:16.560
jointly critiquing and collectively
evaluating student outcomes.

27
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.110
This type of interdisciplinary
approach is unique within art and

28
00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:25.661
design, as they are often conflated
but segregated into separate

29
00:02:25.661 --> 00:02:30.140
courses in reality after the
first-year foundations curriculum.

30
00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.853
The first myth is that graphic
design has nothing to do

31
00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:37.100
with art. Art and graphic
design have similar intentions.

32
00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.782
Until recently, design
was vastly accepted as

33
00:02:40.782 --> 00:02:44.500
utilitarian and pragmatic,
devoid of emotional content.

34
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.910
I don’t agree with this attempt at removal

35
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.320
of emotional connections with an audience.

36
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.423
It’s counterintuitive to what
we call branding and now user

37
00:02:53.423 --> 00:02:56.900
experience, or my preferred
term, human-centered design.

38
00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.944
Branding is building a relationship
with an audience, the individuals

39
00:03:00.944 --> 00:03:03.800
that form an emotional bond to
a certain product or service.

40
00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.460
Although with branding, there is explicit
intent to sell a product or service.

41
00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.026
With human-centered design, there is much

42
00:03:11.026 --> 00:03:13.780
involvement with the
end use of an artifact.

43
00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.917
This is an opening to
incorporate an artistic

44
00:03:16.917 --> 00:03:19.580
expression as well as
design for social justice.

45
00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.560
The second myth is that art has nothing to

46
00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.840
do with design because it is too personal.

47
00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.700
It is viewed through the lens that
art is only a personal expression

48
00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.860
of one’s self and directly linked
to the individual making the work.

49
00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:35.641
The work swings between becoming
overly emotional or non-emotional,

50
00:03:35.641 --> 00:03:39.140
either way devoid of deeper,
richer motivations and interests,

51
00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.580
be it cathartic or aesthetic
value, or art for art’s sake.

52
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.838
Choosing to only look at
art-making as primarily,

53
00:03:46.838 --> 00:03:49.240
if not the sole, part
of the artist’s practice

54
00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.916
limits the artist to produce
watered-down work, and

55
00:03:52.916 --> 00:03:56.520
divorcing personal work from
more universal ideas of design

56
00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:02.000
limits the work to insularity viewpoints
within limited viewing potential.

57
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:46.500
[Closing titles]
